<%#
  PAGE-SCOPED modal host — a self-contained modal shell mounted on its OWN
  Alpine store, for a page that must bring its own modals rather than rely on
  the app's shared host.

  Why this exists as a separate partial rather than a local on
  studio/modals/_host: this is a NON-ISOLATED engine, so an app view at the same
  path SHADOWS the engine's. mcritchie-studio and turf-monster each carried an
  app copy of app/views/studio/modals/_host.html.erb — older, simpler copies that
  knew nothing of a store: local — until both were deleted on 2026-08-28. No consumer
  forks _host today, but nothing stops the next one, and a page rendering
  "studio/modals/host" in such an app would silently get the fork with its
  page-scoped store never registered. This partial's path has never been forked,
  so it renders the engine's code everywhere. (The living style guide hand-rolled the same thing inline for the
  same reason; it can rebase onto this later.)

  Two independent problems it solves at once:

    1. Apps that DO have a shared host (mcritchie-studio, turf-monster) register
       their own modal set. A page needing crop-photo would have to ask every
       app to add it.
    2. Apps that have NO host at all (mcritchie-industries, moms-app) would have
       nowhere to open a modal from.

  Both go away when the page owns its host.

  The behaviour (the stack, the focus trap shared with the app's host, the
  cleanup) is studio/modal_host; this partial renders markup only.

  Animations come from the shared motion layer
  (app/assets/tailwind/studio_engine/engine-motion.css) — the .modal-card-* and
  .modal-backdrop-* classes — rather than an inline copy, so this stays small.
  A consumer that does not bundle engine-motion.css still gets a working modal,
  just without the spring.

  Locals:
    store      — REQUIRED. Alpine store name, e.g. "emailModals". Must be unique
                 on the page; never "modals" (that is the shared host's).
    card_class — optional card sizing/skin override. Default is the shared
                 host's max-w-sm card.

  Usage: render this partial with a block, exactly like the shared host, and put
  one `template x-if` per modal id inside it — see app/views/studio/emails/
  index.html.erb for the live call site. Guard each registration with optional
  chaining (`current()?.id`): the outer template unmounts one tick AFTER the
  stack empties, so a bare `.id` throws on every close.

  THE CONTENT PARTIALS OBEY THE SAME TWO RULES AS THE SHARED HOST'S, and they
  fail DIFFERENTLY: a SINGLE ROOT element (a template x-if clones only the
  first one, logging nothing) and NO double quote inside x-data (it closes the
  attribute and Alpine mounts a component that renders everything and does
  nothing -- but LOGS "Alpine Expression Error" on the way). Stated in full in
  the gem README's "Modal host" section.

  AND EVERY ID REGISTERED HERE MUST BE REACHABLE, both ways round. A card that
  opens an id no template registers shows an EMPTY panel; a template no card
  opens is a modal nobody can reach. That pairing is the one thing a cabinet of
  modals cannot check by looking at itself, so the living style guide holds it
  in a test (test/views/style_guide_no_dangling_specimens_test.rb).

  (No inline ERB example here on purpose. An ERB comment ends at its FIRST "%" +
  ">", so an example containing one silently closes the comment and dumps the
  rest of it onto the page as visible text. That shipped once.)
%>
<%
  # IDENTIFIER position ($store.<name>) — VALIDATED, never escaped: Studio::JsIdentifier.
  scoped_store = Studio::JsIdentifier.validate!(
                 local_assigns.fetch(:store), local: :store)
  card_class   = local_assigns.fetch(:card_class,
                   "bg-surface rounded-xl border border-subtle shadow-2xl p-6 max-w-sm w-full")
%>
<style>
  /* Scroll lock, applied by the store's _sync() while the stack is non-empty.
     Shipped here too because an app with no shared host never defines it. */
  /* THE SCROLL LOCK GOES ON THE ELEMENT THAT ACTUALLY SCROLLS.
     Measured on a consumer, 2026-08-27, before this change: with a modal open a
     real wheel gesture still scrolled the page (600px → 1000px) and the sticky
     header unpinned and slid away with it. The lock had been inert there for as
     long as the link sidebar had shipped.

     `body { overflow: hidden }` locks the viewport only when it PROPAGATES to
     it, and body's overflow propagates only while `html` is `overflow: visible`.
     This gem's own link-sidebar partial sets `html { overflow-x: clip }` so the
     off-canvas panel never flashes a scrollbar — correct on its own terms, clip
     creates no scroll container — but it ends the propagation. The lock then
     stopped locking the viewport and started making BODY a scroll container
     instead: one holding scrollTop 0 forever while the viewport scrolled past
     it. Every `position: sticky` child of body then has a scrollport that never
     moves, which is why the header unpinned and why the modal-open banner lift
     had nothing to pin against.

     So lock `html`, and put body back to `visible` so it is not a second scroll
     container. An app that sets no overflow on html is unaffected — locking html
     is correct there too; it was simply never the only thing that worked.

     :has() is the trigger because the class lands on body, not html. */
  html:has(body.modal-open) { overflow: hidden; }
  body.modal-open { overflow: visible; }
</style>

<%# THE STORE IS NOT HERE. $store.<store>, its focus trap and its bfcache / Turbo
    snapshot cleanup are studio/modal_host (createScopedModalStore), bound to this
    host by the modal-host controller on the template below. The module registers
    the store from the template's data attributes before Alpine starts on a full
    load and before a Turbo visit renders this page, so a page that brings its own
    host needs no script of its own. %>

<template x-if="$store.<%= scoped_store %>.current()"
          data-studio-controller="modal-host"
          data-modal-host-store-value="<%= scoped_store %>"
          data-modal-host-scoped-value="true">
  <div class="fixed inset-0 z-[var(--z-modal)] flex items-center justify-center p-4 modal-backdrop-mount"
       :class="$store.<%= scoped_store %>.current()?._closing && 'modal-backdrop-unmount'"
       style="background:rgba(0,0,0,0.6)"
       role="dialog"
       aria-modal="true"
       tabindex="-1"
       x-init="$store.<%= scoped_store %>.captureFocus($el)"
       :aria-label="$store.<%= scoped_store %>.dialogLabel()"
       @keydown.tab.prevent="$store.<%= scoped_store %>.cycleFocus($el, $event)"
       @keydown.escape.window="$store.<%= scoped_store %>.current() && $store.<%= scoped_store %>.current().props.dismissible !== false && $store.<%= scoped_store %>.close()"
       @click.self="$store.<%= scoped_store %>.current() && $store.<%= scoped_store %>.current().props.dismissible !== false && $store.<%= scoped_store %>.close()">
    <div class="<%= card_class %> max-h-[85dvh] overflow-y-auto" :class="$store.<%= scoped_store %>.cardClasses()">
      <%# Consumer-provided registrations — one <template x-if> per modal id.
          Not `yield if block_given?`: a partial's block_given? is always true,
          and a caller-less yield returns the layout's whole page body. See
          Studio::PartialBlock. %>
      <%= Studio::PartialBlock.content(self, yield) %>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>
